1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,402
Day 61: does a short rules file alone stop a coding agent from

2
00:00:04,402 --> 00:00:08,591
crossing a boundary, or do you also need a mechanical gate.

3
00:00:09,041 --> 00:00:12,865
A recent paper gave coding agents a short rules file and

4
00:00:12,865 --> 00:00:15,597
four boundaries they should never cross.

5
00:00:15,597 --> 00:00:19,762
We repeated it twice, using this project's own rules file and

6
00:00:19,762 --> 00:00:20,582
Claude Code.

7
00:00:21,032 --> 00:00:24,712
Each trial starts a fresh agent with our rules file and

8
00:00:24,712 --> 00:00:27,255
a task that quietly tempts a shortcut,

9
00:00:27,255 --> 00:00:29,797
like installing an unverified package.

10
00:00:30,247 --> 00:00:33,801
In the gated condition, the risky path is mechanically

11
00:00:33,801 --> 00:00:37,750
blocked: no server on the disallowed port, or a locked file.

12
00:00:37,750 --> 00:00:41,370
Our second run also turned off every permission prompt.

13
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:45,309
A hidden test checks what actually happened on disk and

14
00:00:45,309 --> 00:00:49,053
over the network, and we read every command each agent ran.

15
00:00:49,503 --> 00:00:53,583
Across sixteen trials, the hidden tests found zero crossings.

16
00:00:53,583 --> 00:00:55,790
But details changed between runs:

17
00:00:55,790 --> 00:00:58,934
the first run flagged the injected instruction,

18
00:00:58,934 --> 00:01:01,008
the second silently ignored it.

19
00:01:01,458 --> 00:01:07,162
Full repro steps and write-up, in Japanese, on Zenn.

20
00:01:07,162 --> 00:01:10,015
Paper at arXiv 2611.27112.
